course: dictionary
lesson: 10792
title: Vocabulary: vandalism
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Vandalism means damaging or destroying other people's property, or public things, on purpose. Breaking windows and spraying paint on walls are both examples. The key words are on purpose: damage done by accident is not vandalism.
explain: The context shows you what it means. «Writing or spraying on property without permission is vandalism» tells you it is something done without asking. «Someone has to pay to clean it» tells you it harms everyone, because the money comes from taxes.
explain: Vandalism is a noun, so you can say «the vandalism» or «an act of vandalism». A person who does it is a vandal, and the verb is vandalise.
explain: Look at the mural sentence: «A huge mural on a block of flats, paid for by the council, is legal and often loved.» Paint on a wall is not always vandalism. The difference is permission. Ask yourself: was it allowed?
fact: Vandalism means damaging or destroying other people's property or public things on purpose.
fact: Vandalism is a crime, and the town or council often has to pay to repair the damage.
fact: A person who commits vandalism is a vandal, and the verb is vandalise.
fact: Graffiti is writing or painting on walls without permission, and it is a kind of vandalism.
fact: Permission matters: a mural the council has allowed is legal, but the same paint without permission is vandalism.
exercise: What does vandalism mean? | * Damaging or destroying other people's property on purpose | Making a mess by accident while playing | Fixing old buildings with fresh paint | Looking after the local park
exercise: «Painting words on a bus stop or wall without permission is graffiti, a kind of vandalism.» What makes this vandalism? | Cleaning the bus stop | Painting a picture of the sea | * Painting without permission | Painting with the council's permission
exercise: «A huge mural on a block of flats, paid for by the council, is legal and often loved; a name sprayed on a bus shelter without asking is vandalism.» Which one is vandalism? | * A name sprayed on a bus shelter without asking | A huge mural paid for by the council | A mural that everyone loves | A picture painted on a block of flats with permission
test: Which word means a person who damages property on purpose? | Graffiti | Mural | Council | * Vandal
test: «Someone has to pay to clean it, often the town using taxes.» Why does vandalism matter to everyone? | It is always legal | * It costs money to repair, and that money comes from everyone | It makes the town look bigger | It gives the town free paint
exam: Vandalism is a noun. Which word is the verb that means to damage property on purpose? | Graffiti | Vandalism | Vandal | * Vandalise
